<mujoco model="so101 AR scene">
  <!-- AR composition, the twin of assets/franka/ar_scene.xml: the unmodified
       Menagerie so101.xml on a simple table whose top sits at z=0 (the robot
       base plane), extending down 0.73 m to the user's physical floor
       (frames.h maps stage y=0 to MJ z=-0.73). No skybox, no ground plane:
       the background is passthrough. The table is smaller than the Franka's
       because the arm is: the SO101's reach is ~0.35 m, not ~0.85 m. -->
  <include file="so101.xml"/>

  <!-- THE ROBOT SITS AT THE ORIGIN, exactly as the Franka does, and this is a
       decision rather than an omission.

       A verified alternative exists and was deliberately not taken. Putting
       the arm in a <frame pos="..."> requires <attach> instead of <include>,
       and <attach> under MuJoCo 3.10.0's default conflict policy silently
       DISCARDS ALL SIX authored <option> fields of so101.xml — timestep
       0.005->0.002, integrator implicitfast->Euler, cone elliptic->pyramidal,
       impratio 10->1, iterations 10->100, ls_iterations 20->50. Measured, not
       feared. THE PAIR THAT MATTERS IS cone/impratio, the gripper's authored
       friction model: so101.xml's collision_gripper class is condim=6 with
       torsional friction, and elliptic cone + impratio=10 is what makes that
       pinch hold. Lose those and the arm looks identical and grasps
       differently. (The other four are not equally harmless but are far less
       sharp: iterations/ls_iterations measured at 0.000 mm of trace
       difference on this scene — solver headroom this model never spends.)

       <compiler conflict="merge"/> restores all six with zero restatement and
       a bitwise-identical 400-step trace — also measured. It is NOT used here
       because moving the robot is a WHOLE-APP comfort question, not an SO101
       one: at the shipped calibration the Franka's home TCP is 1.592 m from
       the eye and the SO101's is 1.493 m, so the placement argument indicts
       the Franka HARDER, and fixing it for one scene alone is incoherent.
       Nobody has run either scene on a headset yet.

       TRIGGER, so this is a decision with an expiry and not a shrug: the
       first headset session that reports the robot reads too small or too far
       away -> add <compiler conflict="merge"/> and wrap the <attach> in a
       <frame pos="...">. Two constraints come with it, both measured: the
       wrapper must then never author an <option> field (merge switches to
       min/max semantics for any field BOTH files set), and conflict="merge"
       is GLOBAL to the compile, not scoped to one <attach>. Do not reach for
       conflict="error" as a guard — it is documented in XMLreference.rst but
       does not error in 3.10.0; it warns and keeps the parent value. -->

  <worldbody>
    <geom name="table" type="box" size="0.32 0.30 0.365" pos="0.10 0 -0.365"
        rgba="0.46 0.36 0.25 1"/>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- so101.xml ships no keyframe, and src/ik_dls.c requires one named "home"
       (it is both the A-reset pose and, where ns_gain != 0, the nullspace
       bias — though this robot's row sets ns_gain = 0; see src/robot_spec.c).

       The four arm-plane joints are /code/Lab's Isaac-Stack-Cube-SO101 reach
       posture verbatim: they ARE that posture, and their sum is its 45.8 deg
       approach pitch. wrist_roll = pi/2 is NOT from Lab.

       IN JOINT SPACE that deviation is free: wrist_roll is distal to all four
       arm-plane joints, so it changes the jaw orientation without disturbing
       the reach posture at all. IT IS NOT FREE IN THE TASK SPACE, and the
       difference is worth stating because it is easy to assume otherwise. It
       is a spin about the WRIST axis, and the TCP does not lie on that axis:
       tcp_offset[0] = 0.012 puts it 12.0 mm off. So the TCP sweeps a 12 mm
       circle. Measured against Lab's wrist_roll = 0, TCP displacement is
       9.186 mm at pi/4, 16.973 mm at pi/2 (shipped) and 24.004 mm at pi —
       exactly sqrt(2)*12 and 2*12. THE SHIPPED HOME TCP THEREFORE SITS 17.0 mm
       FROM LAB'S, which is 7.6x the pos_med this scene's gate records. If you
       are comparing poses against Lab, that offset is expected and is here.

       Why pi/2 anyway: it rolls the jaws toward level, which is the
       orientation a table-top pick needs. PIN THE DEFINITION BEFORE QUOTING A
       NUMBER — "pinch axis" has at least three readings and they disagree.
       Measured here as the jaw-tip SEPARATION axis (centroid of the three
       fixed_jaw_sph_tip* geoms to the centroid of the three
       moving_jaw_sph_tip* geoms, at the home aperture of 129.9 mm), it goes
       from 80.0 deg off horizontal at wrist_roll = 0 to 26.9 deg at pi/2.
       Under the instantaneous CLOSING direction instead — d(separation)/d(jaw
       qpos) at that same aperture — it is 44.9 deg and 45.8 deg, i.e. barely
       moves, because that direction lies close to the wrist axis it is being
       rolled about. The separation axis is the one the sentence above means.
       An earlier version of this comment quoted "52-88 deg -> 0.7-21 deg",
       which reproduces under none of these definitions; it is gone rather
       than re-derived, and NONE OF THIS HAS BEEN CHECKED ON A DEVICE.

       qpos[gripper] MUST equal robot_spec.c's gripper_open (1.745). teleop.cc
       drives the jaw to gripper_open at trigger = 0, so any other value here
       makes every A-press twitch the jaw as it snaps to the commanded one. -->
  <keyframe>
    <key name="home" qpos="0 -0.6 0.8 0.6 1.5708 1.745"
         ctrl="0 -0.6 0.8 0.6 1.5708 1.745"/>
  </keyframe>
</mujoco>
